 **Pitfalls and Edge Cases:**
-*   **Boundary Artifacts:** At volume boundaries, coverage is sparse, and accumulated weights $W$ are smaller than one. Dividing by small $W$ causes unnaturally high density values. The solution is to threshold the weight at 1 before division [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Ghost Points Alternative:** An alternative solution is to add "ghost points" with zero density and normal weight values around boundaries, mimicking regular volumes with infinite coverage. However, this requires additional data management, whereas the thresholding method is self-contained [S1, unofficial].
